"Encapsulation – Canadian Conservation Institute (CCI) Notes 11/10 - Canada.ca (https://www.canada.ca/en/conservation-institute/services/conservation-preservation-publications/canadian-conservation-institute-notes/encapsulation.html)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.canada.ca/en/conservation-institute/services/conservation-preservation-publications/canadian-conservation-institute-notes/encapsulation.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 306\nL0:   * cite0†Skip to main content L1:   * cite1†Skip to \"About government\" L2: \nL3: ## Language selection\nL4: \nL5:   * cite2†fr (Français) L6: \nL7: cite3†/ Gouvernement du Canada L8: \nL9: ## Search\nL10: \nL11: Search Canada.ca [Input: Search Canada.ca]\nL12: \nL13: Search\nL14: \nL15: * * *\nL16: \nL17: ## Menu\nL18: \nL19: Main Menu\nL20: \nL21: ## You are here:\nL22: \nL23: # Encapsulation – Canadian Conservation Institute (CCI) Notes 11/10\nL24: \nL25: (cite4†PDF (Portable Document Format) Version , 1.22 MB (Megabyte))\nL26: ## CCI (Canadian Conservation Institute) Note 11/10 is part of cite5†CCI (Canadian Conservation Institute) Notes Series 11 (Paper and Books)\nL27: ### Introduction\nL28: \nL29: Encapsulation is a safe and easy procedure commonly used by many museums and archives for storing and protecting frequently handled paper artifacts, such as documents, manuscripts, maps, and plans. Encapsulation has several advantages:\nL30:   * It allows the artifact to be easily handled and viewed through the transparent encapsulation material.\nL31:   * It protects the artifact against potential physical damage during display, study, or storage. It is readily and easily reversible.\nL32:   * It is readily and easily reversible.\nL33:   * It is easy to apply.\nL34: ### Characteristics of Polyester Film\nL35: Polyester film used in encapsulation should be free of plasticisers, surface coatings, and ultraviolet (UV) absorbers^{cite6†Endnote 1 }. Archival polyester film (polyethylene terephthalate), such as Melinex 516, is a transparent, strong, inert, and durable material suitable for use in encapsulation. Various thicknesses are available.\nL36: The thickness chosen will largely depend on the size of the artifact: 3 mil (thousandth of an inch) (0.003\") is standard for most documents and manuscripts, while 7 mil (thousandth of an inch) (0.007\") is more usual for heavier items such as large maps.\nL37: ### Artifacts Not Suitable for Encapsulation\nL38: \nL39: Because polyester film creates an electrostatic charge, encapsulation is not suitable for artifacts with friable media (e.g., charcoal, chalk, pencil, or pastel drawings; gouache) that will transfer onto the film. Artifacts that are attached to a backing board, that are distorted (i.e., cockled or warped), or that show signs of biological infestation by mould or insects should not be encapsulated.\nL40: ### Preparing Artifacts for Encapsulation\nL41: Before encapsulating any artifact, remove loose surface dirt using appropriate erasing compounds (consult CCI (Canadian Conservation Institute) Technical Bulletin Nº (Number) 11, Dry Methods for Surface Cleaning of Paper). Ensure that all fastening devices, such as paper clips, staples, and pins, are also removed. If an artifact is torn but not fragmented, it may be possible to encapsulate it without repairing it.\nL42: Studies have indicated that some acidic papers may deteriorate more quickly if they are not deacidified before encapsulation^{cite7†Endnote 2 }. A paper conservator should be consulted about any artifacts that may require treatment.\nL43: ### Sealing\nL44: \nL45: There are several ways of sealing polyester film. The most common method uses double-sided tape.\nL46: The brand most commonly used is 3M Scotch Brand double-sided tape #415. This tape is pressure-sensitive, and is normally used in 6.4 mm (millimetre) (1/4\") width. The drawbacks of using tape are that the adhesive is acidic, even when new, and has a tendency to \"creep\" (i.e., ooze out) so that if the artifact slips in the polyester envelope, the edge may become attached to the adhesive. As a precaution, apply the tape at least 3 mm (millimetre) (0.12\") away from the artifact.\nL47: The advantage of using tape is that it is relatively inexpensive. However, should the envelope become wet (e.g., in a flood), the two layers of polyester film may separate.^{cite8†Endnote 3 }\nL48: Polyester film cannot be fused using conventional heat sources. Various special sealing machines are commercially available.^{cite9†Endnote 4 }\nL49: #### Ultrasonic welding machines:\nL50: \nL51: These machines convert an electrical impulse into mechanical vibration. The heat generated from this vibration softens the polyester and allows the film to fuse together. Although these machines are expensive, they avoid the use of adhesive tape and they allow many documents to be encapsulated quickly and easily.\nL52: #### Heat-sealing machines:\nL53: \nL54: Several models of heat-sealing machines seal polyester film. One of these machines is also available in a table-top version, which may be suitable for documents of small dimensions.\nL55: ### Making a Polyester Envelope Using Double-Sided Tape\nL56: \nL57: Prior to encapsulation, assemble the following tools and materials:\nL58: \nL59:   * polyester film\nL60:   * double-sided tape (3M #415) 6.4 mm (millimetre) (1/4\") width\nL61:   * grid (graph paper)\nL62:   * small weight\nL63:   * scissors\nL64:   * sharp knife\nL65:   * ruler\nL66:   * small squeegee/cheesecloth\nL67:   * cutting surface (rubber-like self-healing cutting board)\nL68:   * nail clippers\nL69: Select the weight of polyester film most suitable for the artifact that is to be encapsulated. Cut two pieces at least 5 cm (centimetres) (2\") larger all around than the overall size of the artifact.\nL70: \nL71: Place one sheet of polyester film on the grid. The grid makes it easier to align the polyester film, the tape, and the document.\nL72: Place the artifact in the centre of the polyester film, aligning it with the edges of the grid lines. At this point, it is often necessary to hold the artifact in place with a small weight (Figure 1).\nL73: \nL74: cite10†Image: Artifact on polyester film with weight. L75: Figure 1. Position artifact on polyester film. Hold it in place with a weight.\nL76: Place double-sided tape along all four sides of the polyester film, about 3 mm (millimetre) (0.12\") from the edges of the artifact. Align the tape with the edges of the artifact, and leave a small gap at the corners ^{cite11†Endnote 5 } (Figure 2). If no space is left, air will be trapped inside the envelope and the corners will tend to pucker. Take particular care to keep the tape straight when working with long artifacts (e.g., maps and plans).\nL77: Remove the weight, and place the second sheet of polyester film over the artifact (Figure 3). Replace the weight.\nL78: \nL79: cite12†Image: Using tape on artifact. L80: Figure 2. Align tape with edges of artifact. cite13†Image: Another sheet of film over artifact. L81: Figure 3. Place second sheet of polyester film over artifact.\nL82: Remove the paper covering the tape, one strip at a time. Smooth out the polyester film with a small squeegee (Figure 4) or with a soft cloth (e.g., cheesecloth) that will not scratch the polyester film. This not only ensures that the tape is well adhered to the polyester film, but also squeezes out the air.\nL83: \nL84: cite14†Image: Smoothing out film. L85: Figure 4. Remove paper from tape and smooth out film.\nL86: Once all four edges are adhered, use a knife and a straight edge to trim the envelope approximately 3 mm (millimetre) (0.12\") from the edge of the tape; a sharp pair of scissors can be used as an alternative (Figure 5).\nL87: \nL88: cite15†Image: A knife and a straight edge trimming the envelope. L89: Figure 5. Trim envelope.\nL90: \nL91: Round the corners, using nail clippers or scissors, to prevent the sharp edges from damaging other artifacts.\nL92: ### Suppliers\nL93: #### Polyester film (Melinex 516):\nL94:   * Transilwrap of Canada Ltd\nL95: 333 Finchdene Square\nL96: Scarborough, Ontario\nL97: M1X 1B9\nL98: telephone: 416-292-6000\nL99:   * Cadillac Plastic Ltd\nL100: (Distributor for Transilwrap)\nL101: 91 Kelfield Street\nL102: Rexdale, Ontario\nL103: M9W S4A\nL104: telephone: 416-249-8311\nL105: Branches: Dorval, Vancouver, London, Halifax, Edmonton, Calgary\nL106:   * Commercial Plastics & Supply\nL107: 47 Survey Crescent\nL108: Toronto, Ontario\nL109: M6B 1S9\nL110: telephone: 416-787-4215\nL111: Branches: Ottawa, Montreal, Vancouver\nL112:   * Dupont Division Canada\nL113: P.O. Box 26\nL114: Toronto Dominion Centre\nL115: Toronto, Ontario\nL116: M5K 1B6\nL117: telephone: 416-362-5621\nL118:   * Carr McLean\nL119: 461 Horner Avenue\nL120: Toronto, Ontario\nL121: M8W 4X2\nL122: telephone: 416-252-3371\nL123:   * University Products of Canada\nL124: 6535 Millcreek Drive, Unit 8\nL125: Mississauga, Ontario\nL126: L5N 2M2\nL127: telephone: 416-858-7888\nL128: toll-free: 1-800-667-2632\nL129: fax: 416-858-8586\nL130:   * Bury Media & Supplies Ltd\nL131: (Distributor for University Products)\nL132: B-5, 4255 Arbutus Street\nL133: Vancouver, B.C.\nL134: V6J 4R1\nL135: telephone: 604-731-3439/5838\nL136: fax: 604-736-7492\nL137:   * Lowe-Martin Co. Inc.\nL138: P.O. Box 8707\nL139: Ottawa, Ontario\nL140: KIG 3M4\nL141: telephone: 613-741-0962\nL142:   * Norcal Reprographics (1981) Ltd\nL143: 1180 Winnipeg Street\nL144: Regina, Saskatchewan\nL145: S4R IJ6\nL146: telephone: 306-757-5963\nL147:   * Woolfitt's Art Enterprises Inc.\nL148: 390 Dupont Street\nL149: Toronto, Ontario\nL150: M5R 1V9\nL151: telephone: 416-922-0933\nL152: fax: 416-922-3017\nL153: or\nL154: Calgary, Alberta\nL155: Telephone: 403-278-0565\nL156: fax: 403-278-2050\nL157: #### 3M #415 DonbIe-sided tape (1/4\" width):\nL1